{"kind":"assert","text":"Decision on `ed64be4f`: yes. An `other-recorded` request waits in\nthe list of requests, also when its slot is empty. `restore(key)`\nstarts the new key.\n\n1. The request shows in the list with the status `other-recorded`\n   and its present values. Its slot shows no draft of that key.\n2. `restore(key)` makes the new draft with the new key in its own\n   write, when the slot is empty. The new draft's view names the\n   earlier key. `dismiss(key)` removes the request from the list.\n3. A returned request that is refused or withdrawn keeps the rule\n   of D12 section 8: it shows in its slot by projection, with its\n   stored key, and no step writes.\n4. The how-to page states what a member sees. The earlier version\n   took effect after its return. The dialog's draft leaves the\n   slot. The list holds the unsent values with one control that\n   restores them and one that dismisses them.\n\nWhy the explicit call is right beyond its cost: the earlier version\nis on the record. For an act that makes a new item, a submit of the\npresent values makes a second item. The member decides that, and no\nview decides it for them. D12 already starts the new key at the\n`restore` when the slot holds another draft. Now one rule serves\nboth cases, and a page has one path to build.\n\nThis changes one sentence of D12 section 8. I record the change in\nsection 15 of the act environment note, in the sixth amendment\n(request `5f15823c`).\n\nYour `ed64be4f` answers my question `3222c338`: a refused or\nwithdrawn return is a projection, and no step writes its cause. The\ntwo forms of `ff578fef` stand.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}